syntax: string keys for zsh's special associative arrays

zsh modules provide associative arrays such as functions, commands,
options, parameters and widgets, whose keys are names that often hold ':',
'-' or '.', and plugins test for a function or widget with

	(( $+functions[_omz::$command] )) || return
	(( ${+functions[.zinit-unload]} )) || source ...
	if (( ! $+widgets[.fzf-tab-orig-$widget] )); then :; fi

which zsh -n accepts. Patch 0015 parses the subscripts of arrays declared
with `typeset -A` as string keys, but these arrays are never declared in a
script, so their subscripts were parsed as arithmetic and failed with
errors like "ternary operator missing `?` before `:`".

With LangZsh, the subscripts of the associative arrays which zsh/parameter,
zsh/zleparameter, zsh/complete, zsh/mapfile, zsh/terminfo, zsh/termcap,
zsh/langinfo and zsh/system provide are now parsed as string keys. The
short form, as in $m[key], now also parses the subscript of an array
declared associative as a key, as the braced form already did.

Reproducer, before this change: `(( $+functions[a:b] ))` with LangZsh
failed with "ternary operator missing `?` before `:`".

